domain-analysis/circuitBreaker/draft-readme.json
1 What are the full set of states and the exact allowed transitions beyond closed, open, and half-open (e.g., half-open to closed, half-open to open)?
2 What happens when a request succeeds or fails while in the half-open state?
3 How does the CircuitBreaker decide whether to accept or reject a request in each state, beyond the MaxRequests limit for half-open?
4 Is the MaxRequests limit applied exactly as a count of requests, and what is the behavior for additional requests beyond that limit in half-open?
5 What order are OnStateChange and Counts clearing performed in when a state change occurs?
6 Do IsSuccessful and the panic-handling in line 90 interact (is a recovered panic passed to IsSuccessful)?
7 What error does Execute return when the CircuitBreaker rejects the request?
8 What happens if ReadyToTrip is nil and IsSuccessful is nil simultaneously—are defaults independently applied?
9 What exactly constitutes 'more than 5' consecutive failures for the default ReadyToTrip (i.e., is the trip threshold 6)?
10 How are Counts fields (Requests, TotalSuccesses, TotalFailures, ConsecutiveSuccesses, ConsecutiveFailures) updated on each request outcome?
11 What are the states represented by the `State` type referenced in Settings?
12 Is there unspecified behaviour when Interval is nonzero but no requests occur—does clearing still happen cyclically?
C1 18 CircuitBreaker is a state machine intended to prevent sending requests that are likely to fail. supported
C2 19 NewCircuitBreaker creates a new CircuitBreaker from Settings. unclear
C3 41 MaxRequests limits the maximum number of requests allowed to pass through when the CircuitBreaker is half-open. supported
C4 43 If MaxRequests is 0, the CircuitBreaker allows only 1 request. supported
C5 45 Interval is the cyclic period of the closed state for clearing the internal Counts. supported
C6 47 If Interval is 0, the CircuitBreaker does not clear the internal Counts during the closed state. supported
C7 49 Timeout is the period of the open state after which the state becomes half-open. supported
C8 51 If Timeout is 0, the timeout value is set to 60 seconds. supported
C9 53 ReadyToTrip is called with a copy of Counts whenever a request fails in the closed state. supported
C10 54 If ReadyToTrip returns true, the CircuitBreaker is placed into the open state. supported
C11 55 If ReadyToTrip is nil, a default ReadyToTrip is used. supported
C12 56 The default ReadyToTrip returns true when the number of consecutive failures is more than 5. supported
C13 58 OnStateChange is called whenever the state of the CircuitBreaker changes. supported
C14 60 IsSuccessful is called with the error returned from a request. supported
C15 61 If IsSuccessful returns true, the error is counted as a success. supported
C16 62 If IsSuccessful returns otherwise, the error is counted as a failure. supported
C17 63 If IsSuccessful is nil, the default IsSuccessful returns false for all non-nil errors. supported
C18 65 Counts holds the numbers of requests and their successes/failures. supported
C19 77 CircuitBreaker clears the internal Counts either on the change of state or at the closed-state intervals. supported
C20 79 Counts ignores the results of requests sent before clearing. supported
C21 81 CircuitBreaker can wrap any function to send a request via Execute. supported
C22 87 Execute runs the given request if the CircuitBreaker accepts it. supported
C23 88 Execute returns an error instantly if the CircuitBreaker rejects the request. supported
C24 89 If accepted, Execute returns the result of the request. supported
C25 90 If a panic occurs in the request, CircuitBreaker handles it as an error and causes the same panic again. supported
C26 54 A request failing in the closed state causes ReadyToTrip to be called; if it returns true, the CircuitBreaker is placed into the open state. supported
C27 50 After the open-state Timeout elapses, the state of CircuitBreaker becomes half-open. supported
domain-analysis/circuitBreaker/draft-code.json
1 What is the exact initial state of a new CircuitBreaker, and how does toNewGeneration behave given the state is not set before line 177?
2 Line 89 says Interval <= 0 means Counts are not cleared during closed state, but line 153-154 sets interval to defaultInterval (0) for Interval <= 0; is the resulting behaviour consistent with the doc comment?
3 Line 98 says default ReadyToTrip trips when consecutive failures are 'more than 5', while line 193 uses > 5; confirm boundary behavior (5 vs 6).
4 When a panic occurs during req in Execute, afterRequest is called with false (line 237); does this match isSuccessful semantics for panics?
5 Does Allow's returned done callback handle repeated calls or calls after a generation change, and what is the expected behavior?
6 What happens if ReadyToTrip or OnStateChange or IsSuccessful modify or panic, and are they called under the mutex?
7 In State(), currentState may mutate state (e.g., open to half-open) as a side effect; is that the intended semantics of a read accessor?
8 Is the onStateChange callback invoked while the mutex is held (called from setState under lock)?
9 How does the TwoStepCircuitBreaker handle the case where done is never called after Allow returns success?
10 Counts() returns cb.counts by value while holding the mutex, but does not trigger currentState time-based transitions; confirm intended behavior.
C1 16 The package defines three circuit breaker states: StateClosed, StateHalfOpen, and StateOpen. supported
C2 23 ErrTooManyRequests is returned when the state is half-open and the request count exceeds maxRequests. supported
C3 26 ErrOpenState is returned when the circuit breaker state is open. supported
C4 44 Counts are cleared either on a state change or at closed-state intervals. supported
C5 61 A success increments TotalSuccesses and ConsecutiveSuccesses and resets ConsecutiveFailures to 0. unclear
C6 67 A failure increments TotalFailures and ConsecutiveFailures and resets ConsecutiveSuccesses to 0. unclear
C7 85 If MaxRequests is 0, the circuit breaker allows only 1 request in half-open state. supported
C8 89 If Interval is less than or equal to 0, internal Counts are not cleared during the closed state. supported
C9 92 Timeout is the period of the open state after which the state becomes half-open. supported
C10 93 If Timeout is less than or equal to 0, the default timeout is 60 seconds. supported
C11 95 ReadyToTrip is called with a copy of Counts whenever a request fails in the closed state. supported
C12 96 If ReadyToTrip returns true, the CircuitBreaker is placed into the open state. supported
C13 98 The default ReadyToTrip returns true when consecutive failures exceed 5. supported
C14 100 OnStateChange is called whenever the state of the CircuitBreaker changes. supported
C15 103 If IsSuccessful returns true, the error is counted as a success; otherwise it is counted as a failure. supported
C16 105 The default IsSuccessful returns false for all non-nil errors. supported
C17 135 TwoStepCircuitBreaker checks whether a request can proceed and expects the caller to report the outcome via a callback. supported
C18 177 A new CircuitBreaker initializes via a new generation using the current time. supported
C19 189 The default interval is 0 seconds. supported
C20 190 The default timeout is 60 seconds. supported
C21 197 The default IsSuccessful returns true only when err is nil. supported
C22 224 Execute runs the given request if the CircuitBreaker accepts it, otherwise returns an error instantly. supported
C23 226 If a panic occurs in the request, the CircuitBreaker handles it as an error and causes the same panic again. supported
C24 242 Execute calls beforeRequest, then runs req, then calls afterRequest with the isSuccessful result. unclear
C25 284 In beforeRequest, if state is open, ErrOpenState is returned. supported
C26 285 In beforeRequest, if state is half-open and Request count is at least maxRequests, ErrTooManyRequests is returned. supported
C27 289 beforeRequest increments the request count when a request is allowed. supported
C28 299 afterRequest ignores results whose generation differs from the captured generation. supported
C29 313 On success in closed state, counts.onSuccess is called. supported
C30 316 On success in half-open state, counts.onSuccess is called first, then if consecutive successes reach maxRequests, the state is set to closed. supported
C31 326 On failure in closed state, counts.onFailure is called, and if readyToTrip returns true, state is set to open. supported
C32 330 On failure in half-open state, the state is set to open. supported
C33 338 In closed state, if expiry is set and has passed, currentState transitions to a new generation. supported
C34 342 In open state, if expiry has passed, currentState sets state to half-open. supported
C35 349 setState returns early without change if the new state equals the current state. supported
C36 359 setState updates the state, then calls toNewGeneration, then invokes onStateChange if set. supported
C37 365 toNewGeneration increments generation and clears counts. supported
C38 370 In closed state, expiry is set to zero if interval is 0, otherwise to now plus interval. supported
C39 376 In open state, expiry is set to now plus timeout. supported
C40 378 In half-open state, expiry is set to zero. supported
domain-analysis/circuitBreaker/draft-test.json
1 What is the default readyToTrip criterion that causes StateClosed to StateOpen after 6 consecutive failures in the default breaker? The source only shows this behaviour, not the default function's definition.
2 What happens in StateHalfOpen to the Counts after the breaking success that transitions to StateClosed — does any per-request counter observe the third success before counts clear?
3 The custom breaker's OnStateChange is invoked for Closed→Open, Open→HalfOpen, and HalfOpen→Closed; is it ever invoked for other transitions (e.g., HalfOpen→Open or Open-from-tripped-after-timeout) and for what values?
4 In TestCustomCircuitBreaker line 247, assert.True(t, defaultCB.expiry.IsZero()) is asserted on defaultCB, not customCB — is this a test bug or does it reflect intended behaviour?
5 How does expiry behave at creation time: custom settings produce non-zero expiry at line 147, yet no explicit setting of expiry is shown; what initializes expiry?
6 What is the exact semantics of the generation mechanism such that a request from a previous generation has 'no effect' on Counts (line 340)?
7 For TwoStepCircuitBreaker, does done(true)/done(false) get invoked exactly once and what happens on double-done or on panic within the wrapped function?
8 Does StateOpen ever automatically transition to StateHalfOpen without State()/request being called, or is expiry only examined lazily? The pseudoSleep-based tests only observe via State().
9 When StateHalfOpen has already exhausted MaxRequests, what error does the extra request return, and does that error affect Counts?
10 Why does line 247 check defaultCB.expiry instead of customCB.expiry — is the ordering claim about Open→HalfOpen expiry-zeroing for customCB established by any line?
C1 116 State constants are defined as StateClosed=0, StateHalfOpen=1, StateOpen=2. supported
C2 120 State.String returns 'closed', 'half-open', 'open' for the three states, and 'unknown state: 100' for State(100). supported
C3 131 A CircuitBreaker created with empty Settings has empty name, maxRequests 1, interval 0, timeout 60s, non-nil readyToTrip, nil onStateChange, state StateClosed, zero Counts, and zero expiry. unclear
C4 147 A CircuitBreaker created from custom settings retains the configured name, MaxRequests, Interval, Timeout, and a non-nil OnStateChange, starting in StateClosed with zero Counts and non-zero expiry. unclear
C5 153 Negative Interval and Timeout settings are treated as defaults: interval becomes 0 and timeout becomes 60s. supported
C6 172 In StateClosed, consecutive failures accumulate in Counts (Requests, TotalFailures, ConsecutiveFailures) and a following success resets ConsecutiveFailures to 0 while incrementing Successes. supported
C7 182 In StateClosed, reaching 6 consecutive failures trips the breaker to StateOpen and clears Counts to zero, with expiry set non-zero. unclear
C8 186 Requests made in StateOpen return an error and do not change Counts. supported
C9 195 While StateOpen, after 59s of pseudoSleep the state remains StateOpen; after an additional 1s (over Timeout) the state transitions to StateHalfOpen and expiry is zeroed. unclear
C10 199 Sequence: from StateHalfOpen, a single failed request returns the breaker to StateOpen, clears Counts, and sets a non-zero expiry. supported
C11 210 Sequence: after another 60s of pseudoSleep, StateOpen transitions to StateHalfOpen with expiry zeroed; then a success transitions StateHalfOpen to StateClosed with cleared Counts and zero expiry. unclear
C12 234 In the custom breaker, after 29s pseudoSleep a success is counted (Counts{11,6,5,1,0}); after 1 more second (over Interval) a failure resets Counts to {1,0,1,0,1}. supported
C13 242 Sequence in custom breaker: a success then a failure yielding failure ratio 2/3 >= 0.6 trips the breaker from StateClosed to StateOpen, clearing Counts, and invokes OnStateChange with "cb", StateClosed→StateOpen. supported
C14 248 Sequence: pseudoSleeping custom breaker by 90s transitions StateOpen to StateHalfOpen, and OnStateChange is invoked for StateOpen→StateHalfOpen. supported
C15 259 In StateHalfOpen, successes increment Counts up to MaxRequests; when MaxRequests (3) is reached, an additional request returns an error while Counts shows {3,2,0,2,0}. supported
C16 264 Sequence: after the third consecutive success completes in StateHalfOpen, the breaker transitions to StateClosed, Counts cleared, expiry non-zero, and OnStateChange invoked StateHalfOpen→StateClosed. supported
C17 290 TwoStepCircuitBreaker mirrors CircuitBreaker state transitions for failure/success flows: counting failures, tripping at 6 consecutive failures, open-to-halfopen after timeout, halfopen-to-open on failure, and halfopen-to-closed on success. unclear
C18 326 A panic in the requested function propagates as a panic, and Counts records it as a failure ({1,0,1,0,1}). supported
C19 340 A request spanning an Interval boundary (previous generation) does not affect Counts after the new generation starts counting. supported
C20 355 With a custom IsSuccessful returning true, failures are recorded as successes (Counts{5,5,0,5,0}). supported
C21 365 Sequence: after replacing IsSuccessful to require err==nil, 6 consecutive failures trip the breaker to StateOpen. supported
C22 391 Under 10 goroutines each doing 10000 requests concurrently, all succeed and total Counts record {100000, 100000, 0, 100000, 0}. supported
